.\" sermo.1 — man page for sermo (modular sermo)
.\" haplo-dialog <devel@haplo-dialog.fr>
.\" Installed as /usr/share/man/man1/sermo.1
.\"
.TH SERMO 1 "October 2026" "sermo @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "sermo User Commands"
.SH NAME
sermo \- toolkit-agnostic dialog box creator for shell scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sermo
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.br
.B sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.br
.B sermo
\fB\-\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B sermo
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
.br
.B VARIABLE=\(aq<window>...</window>\(aq sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sermo
reads a GUI description written in an XML-like language and displays the
resulting dialog window. It is designed to be called from shell scripts to
present interactive forms, file choosers, progress bars, list views and other
widgets without the script needing any knowledge of GUI programming.
.PP
.B sermo
is the shared front end of a modular suite, which splits the program into a
single hardened core
.RB ( libsermocore ,
parsing and command execution) and one rendering
.I backend
per toolkit. The name
.B sermo
resolves, through
.BR update\-alternatives (1),
to the default backend installed on the system. Backend binaries can also be
called directly:
.BR gtk3sermo ,
.BR gtk4sermo ,
.BR qt6sermo ,
.BR fltk1sermo ,
.BR efl1sermo ,
.BR sdl3sermo ,
.BR lvglsermo ,
.BR ncursessermo ,
.BR htmlsermo .
The XML language and observable behaviour are identical across backends: the
same script renders an equivalent interface on each toolkit.
.B htmlsermo
opens no window: it writes the dialog as a standalone HTML page.
.PP
The GUI description can be supplied from an environment variable
.RB ( \-\-program ),
a file
.RB ( \-\-file ),
or standard input
.RB ( \-\-stdin ).
.PP
When an
.B exit:
action ends the dialog, sermo prints the current value of each named widget
to standard output in the form
\fINAME\fR=\(dq\fIvalue\fR\(dq.
.PP
Nothing else prints them. A
.B closewindow:
action that closes the last window prints only
\fBEXIT=\(dqclosewindow\(dq\fR, and closing the window from the window
manager prints only \fBEXIT=\(dqabort\(dq\fR.
.PP
Those lines escape the four characters the shell expands inside double quotes
.RB ( \e ,
.BR \(dq ,
.B $
and the backtick), so the calling shell may
.B eval
them. Bear in mind that a field's value is typed by whoever
.I uses
the dialog, who is not necessarily whoever wrote the script: if that matters,
prefer
.B \-\-do
instead, which passes the values through the environment and never reads them
back as code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Enable debug mode. Each character read from the dialog description is echoed to
standard output as it is fed to the lexer.
.TP
.BI \-p " VARIABLE" "\fR,\fP \-\-program=" VARIABLE
Read the GUI description from the shell environment variable
.IR VARIABLE .
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-file=" FILE
Read the GUI description from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Read the GUI description from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-\-do= CMD
After the dialog has closed, run the shell command
.IR CMD .
The widget values are exported as environment variables first, so
.I CMD
can read them directly. The command goes through the same path as every other
command the program runs: a direct
.BR exec ()
without a shell when
.I CMD
carries no shell metacharacter, a logged fallback to the shell
.RB ( SERMO_SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
by default) when it does, and a clean refusal when
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK=1
is set.
.TP
.BI \-i " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-include=" FILE
Make the shell functions and variables of
.I FILE
available to every action. Each action's command is turned into
.RB \(dq ". \fIFILE\fP; \fIcommand\fP" \(dq,
so the file is sourced afresh in every action's sub-shell. A relative
.I FILE
is resolved against the directory the program was started from.
.B \-\-do
is the exception: its command is not prefixed.
.TP
.BI \-e " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-event-driven=" FILE
Event-driven program. The shell first reads
.I FILE
.RB ( ". \fIFILE\fP" ),
then the program runs again with
.BI \-\-include= FILE\fR,
keeping
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-print-ir
and
.BR \-\-program .
So
.I FILE
can set the variable that holds the dialog and define the shell functions its
actions call.
.TP
.BI \-g " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-glade-xml=" FILE
gtk3sermo and gtk4sermo only: build the dialog from a GtkBuilder (Glade)
interface file, written for the toolkit's own GTK version; the window is the
object whose id
.B \-\-program
names
.RB ( MAIN_WINDOW
by default), and signal handlers are sermo actions. The other ports refuse the
option and end with status 1.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no-warning
Suppress non-fatal warning messages.
.TP
.BI \-G " GEOMETRY" "\fR,\fP \-\-geometry=" GEOMETRY
Set the initial window placement and size,
.RI [ W x H ][ + X + Y ]
following the X11 convention (e.g. \fB800x600+0+0\fR).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-center
Centre all windows on the screen.
.TP
.B \-\-print-ir
Parse the GUI description, print the internal representation, and exit without
displaying any window. Useful for debugging XML and for headless testing.
.TP
.BI \-\-render-png= FILE
Draw the dialog off screen, write it to the PNG image
.IR FILE ,
and exit. Available on gtk3sermo, gtk4sermo, qt6sermo, fltk1sermo, efl1sermo,
sdl3sermo and lvglsermo. gtk3sermo, gtk4sermo and fltk1sermo still need a
display (a virtual one such as
.B xvfb\-run
will do). ncursessermo draws in a terminal and htmlsermo writes a page: neither
makes an image.
.TP
.BI \-\-space-expand= STATE
Set the default
.I expand
packing state for all widgets
.RB ( true " / " false ).
.TP
.BI \-\-space-fill= STATE
Set the default
.I fill
packing state for all widgets
.RB ( true " / " false ).
.SH WIDGETS
The XML dialog language supports the following tags on every backend (rendered
with native widgets where the toolkit provides them, reimplemented otherwise).
.SS Layout containers
.BR window ", " vbox ", " hbox ", " frame ", " notebook ", " expander ,
.BR eventbox ", " aspectframe ", " grid ", " paned ", " stack ", " flowbox ,
.BR overlay ", " revealer ", " wizard ", " toolbar
.SS Display widgets
.BR text ", " image ", " pixmap ", " statusbar ", " infobar ", " drawingarea
.SS Input widgets
.BR entry ", " edit ", " password ", " searchentry ", " spinbutton ", " hscale ,
.BR vscale ", " checkbox ", " togglebutton ", " radiobutton ", " switch
.SS Selection widgets
.BR combobox ", " comboboxtext ", " list ", " tree ", " table ", " colorbutton ,
.BR fontbutton ", " filechooser ", " calendar
.SS Progress and activity indicators
.BR progressbar ", " pulse ", " spinner ", " levelbar
.SS Button widgets
.BR button ", " linkbutton ", " menubutton .
The
.B button
tag also accepts the stock short forms
.BR "button ok" ,
.BR "button cancel" ,
.BR "button help" ,
.BR "button yes"
and
.BR "button no" .
.SS Menu widgets
.BR menubar ", " menu ", " menuitem ", " separator
.SS Terminal and separators
.BR terminal ", " hseparator ", " vseparator
.PP
The
.B terminal
widget is an interactive shell on gtk3sermo and gtk4sermo (VTE), and on
qt6sermo built with QTermWidget. On the other backends it runs its
.B <input>
command and shows the output, read-only.
.SS Timers
.BR timer
.PP
Each named widget gives its value as a line
\fINAME\fR=\(dq\fIvalue\fR\(dq
on standard output when an \fBexit:\fR action ends the dialog. Nothing is exported to the calling
shell: read the lines with
.B eval
or, safer, use
.BR \-\-do ,
whose command gets the values in its environment.
.SS Backend-specific features
Some capabilities depend on the toolkit. Wayland
.I wlr\-layer\-shell
anchoring (the
.BR layer ", " edge ", " dist " and " reserve
window attributes, for bars and docks) is provided by the GTK 3 backend; on
other backends or under X11 the attributes are ignored and an ordinary window
is shown, leaving the script unaffected.
.SH SOURCES AND TARGETS
.nh
Some tags read a device or a service instead of running a command. The text of
the tag is an all\-in\-one address:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<input modbus>tcp://plc:502?esclave=1&registre=1</input>
<input opcua>opc.tcp://server:4840?noeud=ns=1;s=pieces</input>
<input mqtt>mqtt://broker:1883?sujet=maison/salon/temperature</input>
<input mavlink>udp://:14550?champ=batterie</input>
<input media>media://lounge?donnee=etat</input>
<action mqtt>mqtt://broker?sujet=maison/salon/lampe&valeur=on</action>
<action mavlink>tcp://127.0.0.1:5760?ordre=rtl</action>
<action media>media://lounge?commande=pause</action>
<action media>media://cam?commande=lire&source=v4l2:///dev/video0</action>
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
An
.RB < input >
of this kind only reads: nothing is written to the device.
.IP \(bu 2
.RB < "action mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mavlink" >
and
.RB < "action media" >
are the only writes. They are allowed only in a
.RB < button >
or a
.RB < menuitem >
(a write needs a click); anywhere else the dialog is refused when it is read.
A command that names a
.B sermo\-cible\-
program by hand is refused too.
.IP \(bu 2
The tag becomes a command that runs a helper program,
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\- PROTOCOL
(reading) or
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-cible\- PROTOCOL
(writing). The address is passed as one argument, percent\-encoded, never
through the shell.
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
changes the folder; it must be an absolute path. With
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS ,
name the helper (for example
.BR /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\-modbus ).
.IP \(bu 2
Each helper comes in its own package:
.BR sermo\-source\-modbus ,
.BR sermo\-source\-opcua ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-source\-media ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-media .
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT and MAVLink writes go only to what an allow\-list names:
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt ,
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink .
Without a list, nothing is sent.
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT: credentials
.RB ( identifiants= )
on
.B mqtt://
(not encrypted) are refused before any connection, unless the address carries
.BR clair=oui .
.B mqtts://
requires
.B confiance=
(the authority that signed the server certificate).
.IP \(bu 2
MAVLink: telemetry; a click sets one parameter while the drone is disarmed,
gives a safety order
.RB ( rtl ", " land ,
or
.B terminaison
to cut the motors, which needs a second press within 5 s), or sends a mission
that is never started. No piloting: no arming, takeoff, mode change or
movement.
.IP \(bu 2
Media: sound and video played by mpv. A network source plays only inside
.BR bwrap ;
a local file plays inside
.B bwrap
when it is installed, and otherwise without a sandbox (a message says so).
.IP \(bu 2
Media inside the window: the
.BI dans=$ VAR
key of
.RB < "action media" >
.RI ( VAR ,
the variable of a
.RB < "drawingarea embed=\(dqtrue\(dq" >)
shows the video in that area. gtk3 under X11 only; otherwise (empty variable,
Wayland, another port) the video opens in a separate window, and the message
says so. A
.B dans=
that is not of the form
.BI $ NAME ,
or a value that is not a number, is refused: mpv is not started.
.PP
Details (every parameter, every refusal): the user manual,
.B MANUEL_UTILISATEUR.en.md
section 7.3, in the sources at
.IR https://gitlab.com/haplo\-dialog/sermo .
.hy
.SH EXIT STATUS
sermo exits with status
.B 0
on normal termination. The value printed on standard output by an EXIT action is
a string, not a numeric exit code.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SERMO_DARK
Force the dark or the light variant, overriding system theme detection: a
value that starts with
.B 0
gives the light one, any other non\-empty value the dark one. Otherwise sermo
follows the current system theme. On the GTK backends an explicit
.B GTK_THEME
takes precedence.
.TP
.B GTK_THEME
Override the GTK theme on GTK backends, e.g.
.BR GTK_THEME=Adwaita:dark .
.TP
.B SERMO_INPUT_MAX
The most bytes one
.RB < input >
reads, from a command or a file: 16 MiB (16777216) by default,
.B 0
for no limit. Beyond it, reading stops as if at end of file, and one warning is
printed on standard error, never on standard output. A value that is not a
number of bytes is reported, and the default applies. The progress bar is not
capped: it keeps one line at a time.
.TP
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
When set, refuse the shell fallback (see
.BR SERMO_SHELL )
used for
.RB < input >/< action >
commands that contain shell metacharacters, and fail closed instead. Commands
without shell metacharacters still run via direct
.BR exec ().
.TP
.B SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS
A comma-separated list of commands the program is allowed to run (e.g.
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS=ls,cat,/usr/local/bin/tool ).
Unset by default. Meant for deploying a dialog into a less-trusted context (a
kiosk, a guest session). An entry without a slash allows that name and the path
.B PATH
resolves it to; an entry with a slash allows exactly that path. A program that
bears an allowed name but lives elsewhere is refused. While set, the shell
fallback is refused as well. The tags of SOURCES AND TARGETS run a helper
program: name it in the list.
.IP
The 1.x names
.B HAPLO_ALLOWED_CMDS
and
.B HAPLO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
are still read, with a warning, when the
.B SERMO_
name is not set.
.TP
.B SERMO_SHELL
The shell used for the fallback: the absolute path of an executable program
(for example
.BR SERMO_SHELL=/bin/bash ).
Unset or empty: the default shell,
.B /bin/sh
(under Windows,
.B %ComSpec%
or else
.BR cmd.exe ).
A relative path, or a file that cannot be run, is reported once on standard
error and the default shell is used.
.TP
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
The folder of the helper programs that
.RB < "input modbus" >,
.RB < "input mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mqtt" >...
run (see SOURCES AND TARGETS):
.B /usr/libexec/sermo
by default. It must be an absolute path made of safe characters (letters,
digits and
.BR ._:/=,+@\- );
otherwise the dialog is refused when it is read.
.TP
.B GTKDIALOG_PIXMAP_PATH
Colon-separated list of directories searched for image files not found at the
path given in the XML.
.TP
.B MAIN_DIALOG
Fallback source variable, read when none of
.BR \-\-program ,
.BR \-\-file
or
.B \-\-stdin
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display a minimal dialog from a shell variable:
.PP
.nf
DIALOG='
<window title="Hello">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Hello, world!</label></text>
    <button><label>OK</label><action>EXIT:ok</action></button>
  </vbox>
</window>'
sermo --program=DIALOG
.fi
.PP
Read a dialog from a file and capture the output safely:
.PP
.nf
sermo --file=my_form.xml --do='echo "User entered: $ENTRY"'
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/sermo
The update-alternatives link to the default backend.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gtk3sermo\fR, \fI/usr/bin/qt6sermo\fR, ...
The individual backend executables.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/sermo/
The helper programs of SOURCES AND TARGETS
.RB ( sermo\-source\-* ", " sermo\-cible\-* ),
one package each.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mqtt" >:
yours (mode 600) and root's (mode 644). Without a list, nothing is sent.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mavlink" >,
same rules.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/sermo\-doc/examples/
Example scripts (package
.BR sermo\-doc ).
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B sermo\-gtkdialog
package (which depends on
.BR sermo\-backend\-gtk3 )
makes
.I /usr/bin/gtkdialog
a link to
.BR gtk3sermo ,
so legacy dialogs that invoke
.BR gtkdialog (1)
continue to work unchanged.
